\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ we must have that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\det(A)=0.$* $\\,\\times$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then we must have $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n.$*  $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186529139 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\nI. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nIII. $A$ has $4$ pivot columns" "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $\\det(A)=-1?$ \n\nI. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nIII. $A$ has $4$ pivot columns"] [:db/cas 17592186529139 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{3} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{3} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $\\det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, not $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186594687 :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n*$Z$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of a matrix by a real number) because **addition of matrices in $Z$ is not closed.*** \n\nFor example, consider the matrices\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nin $Z.$ Then,\n\n$$\ndet(A+B)=det\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHence, $A+B\\notin Z.$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- The zero matrix belongs to $Z.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in Z,$ the additive inverse matrix $-A=(-1)\\cdot A$ is also in $Z$ since $det(-A)=(-1)^{2}\\cdot det(A)=1\\cdot 0=0,$ and $A+(-A)=O.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in Z,$ multiplication by a scalar $\\alpha \\in R$ is closed since $det(\\alpha A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot det(A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot 0=0.$ Hence, $\\alpha A\\in Z.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Multiplication of matrices with real entries by a scalar is associative, in general.    $\\,✓$" "The correct answer is \n\n*$Z$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of a matrix by a real number) because **addition of matrices in $Z$ is not closed.*** \n\nFor example, consider the matrices\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nin $Z.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\det(A+B)=det\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHence, $A+B\\notin Z.$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- The zero matrix belongs to $Z.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in Z,$ the additive inverse matrix $-A=(-1)\\cdot A$ is also in $Z$ since $\\det(-A)=(-1)^{2}\\cdot \\det(A)=1\\cdot 0=0,$ and $A+(-A)=O.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in Z,$ multiplication by a scalar $\\alpha \\in R$ is closed since $\\det(\\alpha A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot \\det(A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot 0=0.$ Hence, $\\alpha A\\in Z.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Multiplication of matrices with real entries by a scalar is associative, in general.    $\\,✓$"] [:db/cas 17592186616956 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and  $det(A)=0?$ \n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$" "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and  $\\det(A)=0?$ \n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$"] [:db/cas 17592186616956 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col)}(A)=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null)}(A)=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. A matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=0,$ our matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is *not* invertible, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible,  it must be $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col)}(A)=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null)}(A)=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. A matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $\\det(A)=0,$ our matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is *not* invertible, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible,  it must be $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186617003 :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. All columns of $A$ are pivot columns\n\nIII. $Ax=0$ has no non-trivial solution" "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\det(A)\\ne 0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. All columns of $A$ are pivo