statement?\n\nUse only expressions that refer directly to $A$; powers and inverses of $A$ are outside the requested form."] [:db/cas 17592186272337 :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}} &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}} &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nUse only expressions that refer directly to $A$; powers and inverses of $A$ are outside the requested form."] [:db/cas 17592186446376 :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}} &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}} &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nUse only expressions that refer directly to $A$; powers and inverses of $A$ are outside the requested form."] [:db/cas 17592186435358 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{20}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{2}}=\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(0)}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$" "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{20}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{2}}=\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(0)}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186442802 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $H$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 18 & -3 & 13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 18 & -3 & 13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$ is $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2},{h}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$" "First, we reduce the matrix $H$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 18 & -3 & 13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 18 & -3 & 13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$ is $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2},{h}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$"] [:db/cas 17592186615396 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 13 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 13 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is $\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2},{p}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$" "First, we reduce the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 13 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 13 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is $\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2},{p}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$"] [:db/cas 17592186653551 :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(D),$ we reduce the matrix $D$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 15 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 28\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 28\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 28 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${d}_{1}$ and ${d}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is $\\{{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$" "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(D),$ we reduce the matrix $D$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 15 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 28\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 28\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 28 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${d}_{1}$ and ${d}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is $\\{{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186271988 :question/worked-solution "Recall that by definition, $\\text{nullity}(W)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(W)).$\n\nTo find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(W))$, we reduce $W$ to row echelon form, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 12 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 5 & -20 & 10 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 &